      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix: release stream reader lock, bound error body reads, cancel cloned body",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes three issues where HTTP response bodies are not properly cleaned up or bounded in failure paths:\n\n1. **Ollama stream reader lock never released**: response.body.getReader() lock is never released, relying solely on implicit fetch cleanup which can delay socket reuse in some Node.js versions.\n2. **HTTP error body read without byte limit**: The readResponseBodySnippet fallback uses unbounded response.text(), risking OOM on large error responses from reverse proxies.\n3. **Firecrawl error-path body not explicitly cancelled**: After cloning and consuming a response body for JSON error parsing, the original body is not explicitly cancelled.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\n- ollama/stream.ts: Added reader.releaseLock() in the finally block before release()\n- http-error-body.ts: Replaced response.text() with bounded response.arrayBuffer() + subarray() capping at limits.maxBytes\n- firecrawl-client.ts: Added response.body?.cancel() after successful clone consumption\n\n## User Impact\n\nGateway and plugin processes no longer leak stream resources or risk large memory allocations on error responses. Connection pools recover faster after Ollama/Firecrawl failures.\n\n## Evidence\n\nThree targeted, single-site changes. No modification to success-path behavior.",
